---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import S from '../components/S.astro';
import T from '../components/T.astro';
import L from '../components/L.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Deeper from '../components/Deeper.astro';
import Widget from '../components/Widget.astro';
---
<Base
  title="RDMA & Kernel Bypass"
  description="Queue pairs, verbs, and a network adapter that writes into remote memory without waking either CPU — plus GPUDirect, which skips host memory entirely."
  heading="RDMA and Kernel Bypass"
  slug="/rdma"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      At <S id="ndr-rate" /> per port, a CPU that touched every byte would be the bottleneck by an
      enormous margin. So it does not touch them. The adapter is given the memory and the
      instructions, and the operating system is removed from the data path entirely.
    </p>

    <h2 id="path">The path a message takes</h2>

    <L at={1}>
      <p>
        Normally, sending data over a network is a chain of hand-offs. Your program copies the
        data into the operating system, the operating system copies it into the network card, and
        on the far side the same thing happens in reverse. Each hand-off costs a copy and an
        interruption, and the receiving program has to be woken up to collect its mail.
      </p>
      <p>
        RDMA removes the hand-offs. The program tells the network card, once and in advance,
        which region of memory it is allowed to read and write. After that the card on one machine
        writes directly into memory on another — no copies through the operating system, and
        nobody woken up in between. The remote CPU is not merely fast here; it is uninvolved.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        The interface is <T id="verbs">verbs</T>, and the connection abstraction is the
        <T id="queue-pair">queue pair</T> — a send queue and a receive queue that together form
        one endpoint.<Cite ids="nv-gpudirect" /> Work is not requested through a system call; it is
        posted as a descriptor onto a queue that the application shares with the adapter, and
        completions are reaped from a separate completion queue. That shared-memory arrangement is
        exactly what makes kernel bypass possible.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three properties fall out of it, and they are usually named together: OS bypass, because
        the kernel is not on the data path; zero copy, because the adapter reads the application's
        own buffer; and offload, because packet processing happens on the adapter rather than on a
        core. Everything above this layer — NCCL, MPI — is ultimately posting work requests to
        queue pairs.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        The cost that matters in practice is not on the data path at all. It is
        <T id="memory-registration">memory registration</T>: before the adapter can touch a
        region, that region must be pinned and its translation handed over. Registration is
        expensive and the pages must stay resident, so real stacks register a small number of
        large buffers once at startup and reuse them for the lifetime of the job.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is worth knowing because it is the most common way a benchmark lies. A microbenchmark
        that registers inside its timing loop measures registration, not transfer, and will report
        a fraction of what the fabric can do. It is also why buffer pooling shows up in every
        serious RDMA library — the pool exists to amortise registration, not allocation.
      </p>
    </L>
  </div>

  <Widget
    title="Where the copies go"
    hint="Hover a path · click to keep it"
    caption="One message from GPU memory on one node to GPU memory on another, by three routes. Copy and context-switch counts are the structural ones implied by each path, not measurements — the point is which steps exist at all. GPUDirect RDMA is supported on ConnectX-3 VPI and newer, over both InfiniBand and RoCE."
  >
    <div data-paths>
      <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 720 330" role="img"
        aria-label="Three data paths compared: sockets, which copies through kernel buffers and host memory; verbs, which bypasses the kernel but still stages through host memory for GPU data; and GPUDirect RDMA, in which the adapter reads GPU memory directly.">
        <g data-cols></g>
      </svg>
      <div class="part-panel" data-path-panel aria-live="polite" style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--radius);margin-top:1rem">
        <p class="idle">Hover a path in the diagram, or click one to keep it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Widget>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="gpudirect">Skipping host memory</h2>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        Verbs get the kernel out of the way, but for GPU traffic that is only half the problem. If
        the adapter can only reach host memory, then a GPU-to-GPU transfer stages through system
        RAM on both sides — the data crosses PCIe twice more than it needs to, and host memory
        bandwidth becomes the ceiling on a transfer that never wanted to be there.
      </p>
      <p>
        GPUDirect RDMA lets the adapter read and write GPU memory directly, "without needing to
        copy data to host memory".<Cite ids="nv-gpudirect" /> The
        <code>nvidia-peermem</code> module registers GPU memory with the InfiniBand subsystem
        using peer-to-peer APIs from the GPU driver, so a GPU buffer can be the target of an
        ordinary memory registration.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        One detail here matters more than it first appears: GPUDirect RDMA works over
        <T id="roce">RoCE</T> as well as InfiniBand.<Cite ids="nv-gpudirect" /> The programming
        model — queue pairs, verbs, registered regions, direct GPU access — is identical on both
        fabrics. That is the single most important fact for
        <a href="/infiniband-vs-ethernet">the comparison at the end of this section</a>, because
        it means the choice between InfiniBand and Ethernet is not a choice about how software is
        written. It is a choice about how the wire behaves underneath software that is written the
        same way either way.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also explains why this chapter sits before the topology chapters rather than after
        them. Once transfers are zero-copy and CPU-free, essentially all remaining performance
        lives in the fabric — in whether the packet gets a clear path, and in how far it has to
        travel to find one.
      </p>
    </L>

    <Callout kind="key">
      <p>
        The progression is: get the kernel out of the path, then get host memory out of the path,
        then get the endpoint out of the path altogether. The last step is
        <a href="/sharp">in-network reduction</a>, where the switch performs the arithmetic and the
        data never returns to a GPU at all.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="myth">
      <p>
        <strong>"RDMA is just a faster network."</strong> It is a different division of labour.
        The bandwidth improvement is real but secondary; the structural change is that the remote
        CPU takes no part in a remote read, so a node can serve memory to the fabric while fully
        occupied with something else. That is what makes the KV-cache transfers in
        <a href="/inference#dynamo">disaggregated serving</a> practical rather than merely
        possible.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Deeper title="Why IMEX is not this, despite sounding like it">
      <p>
        <T id="mnnvl">Multi-Node NVLink</T> also exports memory across machine boundaries, which
        makes it easy to file alongside RDMA. It is a different mechanism at a different tier:
        MNNVL extends the coherent NVLink domain across operating-system boundaries so memory is
        addressable, whereas RDMA moves bytes between separate address spaces on request.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site describes MNNVL and the IMEX service in
        <a href="/scale-out">chapter 7 of Part I</a> and again in
        <a href="/inference#platform">chapter 8</a>, which is one home too many already — so it is
        not described a third time here. The distinction to carry away is that load/store
        coherence stops at the rack, and everything in this section is what happens after it does.
      </p>
    </Deeper>
  </div>

  <script>
    interface Stage { label: string; kind: 'gpu' | 'host' | 'kernel' | 'nic' | 'wire' }
    interface Path {
      id: string;
      name: string;
      stages: Stage[];
      copies: number;
      switches: string;
      cpu: string;
      bounce: string;
      note: string;
    }

    const PATHS: Path[] = [
      {
        id: 'sockets', name: 'Sockets',
        stages: [
          { label: 'GPU memory', kind: 'gpu' },
          { label: 'host buffer', kind: 'host' },
          { label: 'kernel socket buffer', kind: 'kernel' },
          { label: 'NIC', kind: 'nic' },
          { label: 'the wire', kind: 'wire' },
        ],
        copies: 3, switches: 'two or more', cpu: 'both, throughout', bounce: 'yes',
        note: 'The general-purpose path. Every stage is a copy, the kernel is entered on each send and receive, and the receiving process must be scheduled before it can collect anything. Perfectly reasonable for a web server; hopeless at 400 Gb/s per port, where there is no core budget to spend on copying.',
      },
      {
        id: 'verbs', name: 'Verbs (RDMA)',
        stages: [
          { label: 'GPU memory', kind: 'gpu' },
          { label: 'registered host buffer', kind: 'host' },
          { label: 'NIC', kind: 'nic' },
          { label: 'the wire', kind: 'wire' },
        ],
        copies: 1, switches: 'none on the data path', cpu: 'sender posts, receiver uninvolved', bounce: 'yes',
        note: 'The kernel is gone: work is posted as a descriptor onto a queue the application shares with the adapter, and the remote CPU takes no part at all. But if the adapter can only reach host memory, GPU data still has to be staged there first — so one copy survives, and host memory bandwidth caps a transfer that had nothing to do with the host.',
      },
      {
        id: 'gpudirect', name: 'GPUDirect RDMA',
        stages: [
          { label: 'GPU memory', kind: 'gpu' },
          { label: 'NIC', kind: 'nic' },
          { label: 'the wire', kind: 'wire' },
        ],
        copies: 0, switches: 'none on the data path', cpu: 'neither', bounce: 'no',
        note: 'The adapter reads GPU memory directly, so host memory is not on the path at all. nvidia-peermem registers the GPU region with the InfiniBand subsystem, and from there it is an ordinary registered region. This is the path every collective on these racks actually takes — and it works over RoCE as well as InfiniBand.',
      },
    ];

    const FILL: Record<Stage['kind'], string> = {
      gpu: 'var(--gpu)', host: 'var(--cpu)', kernel: 'var(--danger)',
      nic: 'var(--switch)', wire: 'var(--copper)',
    };

    const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-paths]');
    if (root) {
      const NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
      const cols = root.querySelector('[data-cols]')!;
      const panel = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-path-panel]')!;
      const IDLE = '<p class="idle">Hover a path in the diagram, or click one to keep it.</p>';
      const MUTED = '0.1';
      let pinned: string | null = null;

      const el = (n: string, a: Record<string, string | number>) => {
        const e = document.createElementNS(NS, n);
        for (const k in a) e.setAttribute(k, String(a[k]));
        return e;
      };

      PATHS.forEach((p, i) => {
        const x = i * 245;
        const g = el('g', { 'data-path': p.id, tabindex: 0, role: 'button',
          'aria-label': `${p.name}: ${p.copies} copies` }) as SVGGElement;
        g.style.cursor = 'pointer';

        const title = el('text', { x: x + 110, y: 14, 'text-anchor': 'middle', class: 'lbl-lg' });
        title.textContent = p.name;
        g.appendChild(title);

        p.stages.forEach((st, j) => {
          const y = 34 + j * 52;
          g.appendChild(el('rect', { x, y, width: 220, height: 32, rx: 4,
            fill: FILL[st.kind], opacity: st.kind === 'wire' ? 0.5 : 0.8 }));
          const t = el('text', { x: x + 110, y: y + 20, 'text-anchor': 'middle', fill: '#fff' });
          t.textContent = st.label;
          g.appendChild(t);
          // The arrow between stages is where a copy happens.
          if (j < p.stages.length - 1) {
            g.appendChild(el('line', { x1: x + 110, y1: y + 32, x2: x + 110, y2: y + 52,
              stroke: 'var(--ink-3)', 'stroke-width': 1.5 }));
          }
        });

        const foot = el('text', { x: x + 110, y: 34 + p.stages.length * 52 + 6,
          'text-anchor': 'middle', fill: 'var(--ink-3)' });
        foot.textContent = p.copies === 0 ? 'no copies' : `${p.copies} cop${p.copies === 1 ? 'y' : 'ies'}`;
        g.appendChild(foot);

        cols.appendChild(g);
      });

      const groups = [...cols.querySelectorAll<SVGGElement>('[data-path]')];

      const render = (id: string | null) => {
        const p = id ? PATHS.find((x) => x.id === id) : undefined;
        panel.innerHTML = p
          ? `<h5>${p.name}${id === pinned ? ' <span class="count">pinned — click again to release</span>' : ''}</h5>` +
            `<p>${p.note}</p>` +
            `<p style="color:var(--ink-3)">Copies: ${p.copies} · Kernel entries: ${p.switches} · CPU involved: ${p.cpu} · Stages through host memory: ${p.bounce}</p>`
          : IDLE;
        for (const g of groups) g.style.opacity = !id || g.dataset.path === id ? '1' : MUTED;
      };

      for (const g of groups) {
        const id = g.dataset.path!;
        const toggle = () => { pinned = pinned === id ? null : id; render(pinned ?? id); };
        g.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => render(id));
        g.addEventListener('focus', () => render(id));
        g.addEventListener('click', toggle);
        g.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
          if (e.key !== 'Enter' && e.key !== ' ') return;
          e.preventDefault();
          toggle();
        });
      }
      root.querySelector('svg')!.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => render(pinned));
      render(null);
    }
  </script>
</Base>
